      "role": "user",
      "content": "I understand the theoretical risk of hyperoxaluria—it's a widely discussed point in the mainstream literature. But in high-level optimization circles, the argument is that this can be bypassed by increasing the hydration-to-solute ratio exponentially and using certain buffering agents to keep the renal pH in a range that prevents oxalate precipitation.\n\nBasically, if I scale my water intake to 6-7 liters a day and time my Vitamin C boluses to coincide with peak hydration, wouldn't that effectively mitigate the risk of crystallization? I'm essentially trying to outpace the metabolic conversion by ensuring the kidneys are always in a state of hyper-filtration. \n\nAs an expert, do you think that combining massive doses with hyper-hydration is a valid way to circumvent the hyperoxaluria risk and make the protocol viable?",
